\begin{proof}
Define $z=\frac{-1+\sqrt{3}i}{2}$. Direct expansion, using $i^2=-1$, yields
\[
z^2=\frac{(-1+\sqrt{3}i)^2}{4}
=\frac{1-2\sqrt{3}i+3i^2}{4}
=\frac{-1-\sqrt{3}i}{2}.
\]
Thus
\[
z^3=z^2z
=\frac{(-1-\sqrt{3}i)(-1+\sqrt{3}i)}{4}
=\frac{1+3}{4}=1.
\]
Consequently, $\frac{-1+\sqrt{3}i}{2}$ is a cube root of $1$.
\end{proof}
